\paragraph{Model, date and investigated scope.}
OpenAI GPT-6 Astra (configured model \texttt{gpt-6-astra}), ultra reasoning; generated 11 October 2026. Computed the two-coupling first-order Hessian determinant and verified an exact rank-one degeneracy. The bounded-fast-forcing asymptotic, positive lower bounds and exponent transition are not proved.

\paragraph{Sources and literature check.}
Read the supplied complete definition, catalogue wording, scope qualifications and source list for this canonical ID. The original-source citations are inherited as supplied references; no fresh primary-source or novelty search was performed for this attempt. No current open-status claim is inferred from this calculation.

\paragraph{A first-order calculation and its limitation.}The question concerns a bounded fast forcing, so expansion at zero in both couplings is only a diagnostic. For the unperturbed positive separatrix choose \(\varphi_0(t)=4\arctan e^{gt}\); then \(\cos\varphi_0-1=-2\operatorname{sech}^2(gt)\). Write \(f(\alpha)=\sum_k f_k\cos(k\cdot\alpha)\), taking one representative of each opposite Fourier pair, and similarly \(m(2\alpha_2)=\sum_l m_l\cos(2l\alpha_2)\). The integral of the perturbing potential along this orbit is
\[
L(\alpha)=-2\varepsilon\sum_k f_k W(k\cdot\omega)\cos(k\cdot\alpha)-2\varepsilon'\sum_l m_l W(2l\omega_2)\cos(2l\alpha_2),\quad
W(v)=\int_{\mathbb R}\operatorname{sech}^2(gt)\cos(vt)\,dt.
\]
All sums are finite, and exponential decay permits differentiation. Thus its Hessian at zero is the real symmetric matrix \(M=2\varepsilon\sum_k f_kW(k\cdot\omega)kk^T+8\varepsilon'\sum_l l^2m_lW(2l\omega_2)e_2e_2^T\), where \(e_2=(0,1)^T\). If the first term is \(\varepsilon C\) and the second is \(\varepsilon'De_2e_2^T\), elementary expansion gives
\[\det M=\varepsilon^2\det C+\varepsilon\varepsilon'D C_{11}.\]
This identifies precisely the two competing terms in this linear-response determinant; it does not identify the determinant of the true splitting for order-one \(\varepsilon'\).
\paragraph{Countercheck and failed extension.}If every Fourier vector of \(f\) is parallel to \(e_2\), then the full Hamiltonian is independent of \(\alpha_1\). Hamilton's equation gives \(\dot A_1=0\); both invariant-manifold graphs have \(A_1=0\), and their splitting determinant vanishes identically whenever those graphs exist. Thus the nondegeneracy proviso cannot be dropped. Even when \(C_{11}\ne0\), cancellation between the two displayed determinant terms is possible. Bounding a higher-order error merely by \(O(\varepsilon^2+\varepsilon'^2)\) is useless against an exponentially small determinant. A uniform error smaller than the actual candidate leading term along the stated Diophantine sequence, with bounded fast forcing, is the first missing step. The source's critical-exponent transition and a positive lower bound remain unresolved; this is a first-order diagnostic, not a new splitting theorem.

